    3. Core Problem and its Causes:
    The participants used the “Problem Tree” analytical methodology to identify the core development
    problem and its underlying causes in the district as follows:
    Poverty and distress is the main problem of the district due to low level of agricultural and livestock
    production levels and quality, low level of infrastructural and social services and occurrence of natural
    disasters and vulnerability of the district residents.
    Roads, culverts and bridges have been destroyed owing to wars and floods and most of villages have no
    access to linking roads. Mines hasn’t been extracted and exploited due to negligence of the government
    and lack of budget. Healthcare services are poor. Clinics are not equipped with modern equipments,
    quality medicines aren’t available, qualified and experienced physicians are not at the access of the
    people. And especially women lose their lives in such situation owing to lack of access to maternal
    clinic in the district. Education is very week. Schools are lacking buildings, quality teaching materials
    and qualified teachers. In education sector, the district faces shortage of school buildings so the children
    study under the open sky in the cold chilly winter as well as hot summer seasons, which discourage the
    students to continue their study and the ones who are interested to go ahead for getting education get
    sick because of these unfavourable weather conditions. Agriculture is the main income source of the
    residents of the district and they have much more agricultural land but unfortunately have no access to
    modern agricultural equipments such as tractors, threshers, chemical fertilizers and quality pesticides.
    Moreover, seasonal floods threaten the district and have destroyed agricultural lands, agricultural
    products and killed a great number of animals. All the above problems paved the way to poverty and
    distress.
    4. Development Goal:
    In order to address the core problem in the district, the participants formulated an overall development
    goal for the district as follows:
    To provide better access to basic infrastructure and social services, improve agricultural and
    livestock productions and reduce the vulnerability rate of the residents against natural disasters
    5. Development Objectives and Strategies:
    In order to achieve the overall development goal, the participants set four development objectives for
    the district and recommended strategies for achieving those objectives.
    3

  -- page 4
    First Objective:
    To improve social services
    Major Strategies:
    • Improvement of literacy rate and education system
    • Improvement of healthcare services
    • Creation of job opportunities for the district residents
    Second Objective:
    To provide access to overall infrastructural services
    Major Strategies:
    • Provision of essential equipments for extraction and exploitation of mines
    • Provision of access to improved roads, bridges and transportations
    • Implementation of city’s master plan
    • Ensuring access to electric energy and relevant facilities
    Third Objective:
    To improve the quality and quantity of agricultural and animal husbandry products
    Major Strategies:
    • Provision of adequate irrigation water and regularised irrigation system
    • Prevention and control of animal diseases and plant pests
    • Provision of mechanized agricultural equipments
    Fourth Objective:
    To decrease the risks of natural disasters and vulnerability of the residents
    Major Strategies:
    • Taking strong action to protect residential houses, agricultural lands, and livestock against
    floods
    • Taking sturdy action to protect agricultural and animal products against unpleasant effect of
    drought
    • Taking action to decrease the vulnerability rate of the residents against earthquake
    6. Prioritized Projects Ideas:
    After having developed strategies, the participants identified and prioritized (66) project ideas to
    achieve the development objectives for the district. The project ideas were discussed in the Provincial
    Development Plan (PDP) to ensure that community priority needs are articulated in provincial and
    national development plans of the Government. The PDP was prepared in July 2007 in a process of sub-
    national consultation and provincial development planning workshops.
    For more details click on www.mrrd-nabdp.org.af
